# Intelligent Scalable Multi-Agent Architecture for Vehicle Engineering Programs

---

## Abstract

Modern vehicle engineering programs operate across multiple tightly coupled domains — Integration, Vehicle Systems, CAB, Electrical & Electronics, Drivetrain, and Engine — each generating high volumes of interdependent design decisions across a compressed product lifecycle. The inherent complexity of coordinating these domains, combined with the computational burden of iterative simulation-driven design, creates a structural bottleneck that conventional engineering workflows are ill-equipped to resolve.

This report presents the design, rationale, and technical implementation of an **Intelligent Scalable Multi-Agent Architecture** conceived specifically for this environment. The framework introduces a hierarchical agent structure anchored by a Central Orchestrator, beneath which a standardized, reusable agent layer serves every engineering subsystem. Each subsystem is equipped with an identical set of purpose-built AI agents — Expert Advisor, Design Calculation, Verification & Validation, DFMEA, and beyond — ensuring deep modularity, consistent domain intelligence, and infinite extensibility across the full program scope.

To demonstrate the concrete engineering utility of this architecture, the report presents a complete technical implementation of one agentic component: **ride comfort optimization for a 6×2 heavy commercial vehicle equipped with a Non-Reactive Suspension (NRS) system**. The vehicle is modeled as a 6-degree-of-freedom multi-body dynamic system capturing cabin bounce, pitch, and roll, together with axle-level inputs from one front and two rear tandem axles. Governing equations of motion are derived from Newton–Euler first principles, and geometric constraints arising from the NRS tandem coupling are enforced through a KKT-based constrained dynamics formulation with Baumgarte stabilization.

Two complementary modeling paradigms are developed and integrated within this framework. The first is a **physics-based ODE model** that solves the full constrained system numerically using the Radau integrator in SciPy, incorporating a two-stage asymmetric nonlinear damper to faithfully represent real-world shock absorber behavior. The second is a **Physics-Informed Neural Network (PINN) surrogate** — a hybrid deep learning model whose loss function directly embeds the governing ODE residuals, ensuring physical consistency throughout training. The surrogate architecture combines a Temporal Convolutional Network (TCN) encoder for road profile processing with a parameter-conditioning MLP and an LSTM decoder, enabling inference in milliseconds against the 10–11 hour runtime of the physics-based solver.

Both models are integrated with two optimization strategies: **Bayesian Optimization** for single-objective convergence and **NSGA-II** for multi-objective Pareto-front exploration. Results demonstrate that multi-objective optimization using NSGA-II achieves superior engineering utility, delivering combined RMS seat accelerations of approximately **0.727–0.738 m/s²** — a reduction of over 30% from the unoptimized baseline of 1.143 m/s² — while simultaneously exposing the trade-off space across bounce, longitudinal, and lateral axes for informed engineering decision-making.

The entire optimization pipeline is structured as an agentic workflow using the **LangGraph** framework, where validation, baseline evaluation, optimization, and result reporting are implemented as self-aware, failure-tolerant nodes in a directed state graph. This architecture is the foundation for a connected, automated, and expert-augmented engineering intelligence layer applicable across all product development subsystems.

