## Conclusion

### Summary of Contributions

This work presents both an architectural framework and a fully realized technical implementation that together establish the foundation for intelligent automation in complex vehicle engineering programs.

At the architectural level, the proposed **Intelligent Scalable Multi-Agent Architecture** addresses a fundamental limitation of current engineering workflows: the absence of a connected, domain-aware intelligence layer capable of operating continuously across all subsystems and lifecycle stages. The framework's hierarchical structure — a Central Orchestrator governing a uniform, reusable subsystem agent layer — ensures that every engineering domain benefits from the same standardized set of AI capabilities without requiring bespoke development for each subsystem. Modularity, scalability, and human-in-the-loop governance are not design aspirations in this framework; they are structural properties enforced by the architecture itself.

At the implementation level, the ride comfort optimization study for the 6×2 NRS-equipped commercial vehicle demonstrates that this architectural vision is not merely theoretical. A complete, end-to-end agentic pipeline was developed and validated, incorporating physically rigorous modeling, surrogate acceleration, multi-strategy optimization, and autonomous workflow management.

### Technical Findings

**Modeling fidelity and constraint enforcement.** The physics-based ODE model, formulated from Newton–Euler equations across six degrees of freedom, accurately captures the coupled bounce, pitch, and roll dynamics of both the cabin-chassis assembly and the tandem rear axle system. The KKT-based constrained dynamics formulation with Baumgarte stabilization proved effective in maintaining the physical consistency of the NRS mechanical coupling throughout numerical integration, preventing the constraint drift that would otherwise render long-duration simulations unreliable.

**Asymmetric damper representation.** The two-stage nonlinear asymmetric damper model provides a significantly more faithful representation of real-world shock absorber behavior than a linear equivalent. Comparative evaluation of the two integration strategies — direct coupled optimization (Option 1) and two-phase sequential optimization with curve fitting (Option 2) — yielded comparable RMS acceleration results, with differences of less than 0.007 m/s² in the combined metric. However, Option 1 is the recommended approach: it operates on the true system without post-processing approximations, maintains physical consistency throughout, and eliminates the sequential dependencies and approximation errors inherent in the curve-fitting phase.

**Optimization strategy selection.** Both Bayesian Optimization and NSGA-II successfully reduced cabin seat RMS acceleration from the baseline of 1.143 m/s². The single-objective Bayesian approach converged to a combined RMS of approximately 1.015 m/s² (a reduction of ~32.8%), while NSGA-II's multi-objective formulation produced Pareto-optimal solutions achieving 0.727–0.738 m/s² combined RMS — reductions exceeding 33% — with simultaneous improvements of 50% in the longitudinal axis and 44–52% in the lateral axis. Critically, NSGA-II's output is not a single point but a trade-off surface, which directly supports engineering judgment: the designer can select the solution that best reflects the program's specific ride comfort priorities rather than accepting a single aggregated optimum that may not align with real-world targets.

**PINN surrogate performance.** The Physics-Informed Neural Network surrogate, built on a TCN–MLP–LSTM architecture, demonstrated consistent convergence across all training metrics. Test evaluation across 20 cases yielded a mean RMS relative error of 5.12%, a maximum of 10.43%, and a seat acceleration correlation of 0.774 with the ODE reference — acceptable fidelity for design space exploration. The trained model reduced optimization runtime from 10–11 hours to minutes with no meaningful degradation in solution quality for configurations within the training distribution. This represents a two-orders-of-magnitude reduction in computational cost, transforming what was a bottleneck into an on-demand design tool. Prediction accuracy is expected to improve further when the synthetic training dataset is replaced with real-world axle measurement data or high-fidelity ADAMS simulation data.

**Agentic workflow integration.** The LangGraph-based implementation demonstrates that the optimization pipeline can be managed autonomously, with each node responsible for its own domain: input validation, baseline establishment, optimization execution, and result validation. The directed state graph structure ensures that failures are caught early — before expensive ODE evaluations are triggered — and that the workflow handles both single-objective and multi-objective modes through conditional routing rather than hardcoded logic. This agentic structure is directly portable to other subsystems without architectural modification.

### Broader Implications for Vehicle Engineering Programs

The implementation presented here is intentionally exemplary rather than exhaustive. Ride comfort optimization is one node in a program-level engineering graph that encompasses hundreds of subsystems, thousands of design parameters, and continuous interactions between domains. The significance of this work lies in demonstrating that the agentic architecture is not blocked by technical complexity: a problem of the caliber presented here — constrained multi-body dynamics, nonlinear damper modeling, surrogate training, multi-objective optimization — can be fully embedded within an autonomous, auditable, and extensible agent.

The same blueprint applies directly to steering system stiffness optimization, drivetrain NVH parameter tuning, thermal management system design, frame structural analysis, and fuel system layout — any subsystem for which a physical model and an objective function can be formulated. The framework does not need to be redesigned for each new domain; it needs to be instantiated with domain-specific knowledge, just as the suspension agent was instantiated here with suspension dynamics, damper models, and ride comfort standards.

### Limitations and Forward Path

Several areas present clear opportunities for refinement and extension.

The surrogate model's current training dataset is entirely synthetic, derived from the ODE solver. While the physics-informed loss provides a regularization mechanism that discourages physically inconsistent predictions, the model's accuracy in regions of the parameter space underrepresented in training data remains bounded. Integration of real-world measurement data from instrumented test vehicles, or high-fidelity ADAMS multibody simulation results, will be a necessary step before the surrogate can be deployed as a production engineering tool.

The current optimization study focuses on four primary suspension parameters. Real engineering programs involve a larger and more interconnected parameter space, including cabin mount properties, anti-roll bar stiffness, tyre characteristics, and load-state-dependent parameters. Extending the optimization framework to accommodate this broader parameter set — while maintaining tractable convergence — will require careful attention to surrogate coverage and optimization algorithm selection.

The agentic architecture currently manages the optimization workflow autonomously but relies on the engineer to define input configurations and interpret Pareto-front solutions. Future development should include intelligent recommendation agents capable of proposing initial configurations based on program history, flagging Pareto solutions that violate regulatory or durability constraints, and surfacing design insights extracted from the optimization trajectory itself.

Finally, the full realization of the Central Orchestrator — the program-level agent responsible for coordinating cross-domain interactions, managing design freeze sequences, and maintaining context across the full product lifecycle — remains the defining engineering challenge of this framework. The subsystem agent layer demonstrated here is the prerequisite for that orchestration layer: it cannot be built until domain-level agents are proven capable of reliable, auditable, autonomous execution.

### Closing Statement

The architecture and implementation presented in this report represent a concrete and technically grounded step toward a new paradigm in vehicle engineering: one where AI agents do not merely assist engineers with isolated tasks, but operate as persistent, connected, domain-expert collaborators across the full product development program. The ride comfort optimization agent is one demonstration of what this layer looks like in practice. The framework is designed so that every subsequent agent — in every domain, at every lifecycle stage — can be built on the same foundation, with the same guarantees of modularity, physical rigor, and engineering accountability.

The path from current practice to that vision is not a single leap. It is the successive, disciplined instantiation of the architecture described here, one subsystem at a time, until the connected intelligence layer becomes the engineering program's operational backbone.​​​​​​​​​​​​
